# HTTP parser abuse-case golden vectors (NET-7.3 hardening).
#
# These pin the parser's defensive limits: a conforming parser must
# REJECT each generated message with the named failure before
# buffering unboundedly. The messages are described generatively (so
# the corpus stays small) and materialized by the loader.
#
# Format (fields '|'-separated):
#   <name> | <generator> | <expected-failure>
#
#   generator grammar (see GoldenVectors.h::generateAbuseCase):
#     header-flood:<count>         -> a request with <count> "X-N: v" headers
#     long-header-line:<bytes>     -> one header value of <bytes> chars
#     long-request-line:<bytes>    -> a request target of <bytes> chars
#     missing-crlf-terminator      -> headers never terminated by a blank line
#     bad-chunk-size:<text>        -> a chunked body whose size line is <text>
#     truncated-body               -> Content-Length larger than the bytes sent
#
#   expected-failure -> the NET-7.7 HttpException subtype name the parser
#                       must raise: HeadersTooLarge | MalformedMessage |
#                       InvalidChunkEncoding | UnexpectedEof
#
header-flood-10k    | header-flood:10000        | HeadersTooLarge
long-header-line    | long-header-line:1000000  | HeadersTooLarge
long-request-line   | long-request-line:1000000 | HeadersTooLarge
no-terminator       | missing-crlf-terminator   | UnexpectedEof
bad-chunk-size      | bad-chunk-size:zzz        | InvalidChunkEncoding
truncated-body      | truncated-body            | UnexpectedEof
